.TH OC-BOX 1 "" "oc-box" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
.PP
oc\-box \- persistent rootless Podman environments for mainline OpenCode.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box [start] [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID] [START\-OPTIONS]
oc\-box stop [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID]
oc\-box sync [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID]
oc\-box reset [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID] [RESET\-OPTIONS]
oc\-box repair [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID]
oc\-box purge [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID] [\-\-delete\-sessions | \-\-all] [\-y]
oc\-box list
oc\-box distros
oc\-box \-\-status [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID] [\-\-json]
oc\-box status [\-\-project PATH | \-\-workspace ID] [\-\-json]
oc\-box \-\-about
oc\-box \-\-version
oc\-box \-h | \-\-help
.fam T
.fi
.PP
The command defaults to \fBstart\fR\&. Place options before or after the command. Run the launcher on the host as your regular user, without sudo. It manages rootless Podman; container root is mapped to the unprivileged host user.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
oc\-box starts unmodified OpenCode and its local MCP servers, plugins, formatters, language servers, shell, and file tools together inside a persistent development container. It associates one environment with one canonical project directory. The entire project, including hidden files, Git history, untracked files, and unfinished edits, is mounted writable at the same absolute path.
.PP
The container HOME has the host user's home pathname but uses a private named volume. It is not a mount of the host home. The live OpenCode database, session IDs, snapshots, caches, and home\-installed dependencies persist in this volume. Packages installed elsewhere in the OS persist in the container's writable layer until that container is reset or removed.
.PP
On normal exit, oc\-box exports conversation history to separate archive sessions in the normal host OpenCode database, then stops the container. Launching oc\-box again resumes the original container session. Running processes do not survive a stop. Host OpenCode and its configuration are unchanged. Build mode permits all OpenCode tool permissions inside oc\-box; Plan mode and subagents retain their own rules.
.PP
The default resource limits are 4 CPU cores, 8 GiB RAM, and 2048 processes. These are ceilings, not reservations: resources are consumed dynamically up to those limits, and OpenCode itself has no fixed 4/8 GiB minimum requirement. There is no disk quota. Containers share the host kernel and can reach network services, including configured host services at \fBhost.containers.internal\fR\&. The project is intentionally writable and can be deleted by container commands. Additional Podman options and devices explicitly extend these defaults.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS start
.PP
Create or resume an environment. This is the default when no command is given. The project directory must already exist and be dedicated to the project. Without a selector, use the current directory. With \fB\-\-workspace\fR, use the registered directory; a workspace ID takes precedence if both selectors are provided. Do not combine selectors when a single selector will do.
.PP
On first launch, detect the host distro, create a labeled container and private home volume, install baseline tools, install the exact host OpenCode release, copy configuration, resolve plugin dependencies, and start a conversation. Afterward, start the existing environment and resume its latest recorded root conversation. \fB\-\-new\fR starts another conversation in the same environment.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
mkdir \-p ~/devel/demo
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box start \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-new
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Cold startup includes image/package/plugin downloads; timed stage messages appear every five seconds. A loading line covers blank TUI startup frames and disappears when OpenCode draws text. Warm launches retain compatible caches. The container OpenCode version is verified against the host on every launch; exact upgrades and downgrades are supported, with no fallback to another release.
.SS stop
.PP
Stop a registered container without deleting the project, container OS, private home, session IDs, or metadata. Already stopped or missing containers need no start. This command can run from a second host terminal while OpenCode is active. Its processes stop, and the active client may disconnect or report tool errors. Exit that launcher before starting another instance or resetting the workspace. Use \fBsync\fR afterward if a final history export was missed.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box stop \-\-project ~/devel/demo
.fam T
.fi
.SS sync
.PP
Refresh host conversation archives without opening the TUI. Requires an existing workspace and an intact project directory. It starts or recreates the container if needed, prepares its runtime/configuration, discovers saved root sessions, exports them, and imports separate namespaced archive copies into the host database. It stops the container afterward, even if it had been running.
.PP
An active oc\-box launcher holds a workspace lock, so exit it before syncing. Use this after an OpenCode crash, missed exit\-time sync, or host restart. Sync failure preserves container data; retry after resolving the error.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box sync \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box sync \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID
.fam T
.fi
.SS reset
.PP
Recreate the environment's container OS, then launch OpenCode and resume work. Exit the active workspace launcher first. Reset removes the owned container and owned networks while retaining the private home/session volume and any other workspace\-owned volumes, project, Git history, unfinished edits, session IDs, and resume metadata. Installed system packages and files outside the project/private home must be recreated; running processes stop. Baseline tools and OpenCode are reinstalled automatically.
.PP
Use reset to change distro, CPU/RAM/process limits, devices, or extra Podman settings. These are not live updates in oc\-box, even though Podman itself can update some limits. No purge is required. Native binaries cached in the home may need reinstalling when switching distributions, especially to/from Alpine.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-cpus 8 \-\-memory 16g
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-distro ubuntu
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-device /dev/accel/accel0
.fam T
.fi
.SS repair
.PP
Explicitly accept the current directory identity for an existing workspace at its original canonical path. Use this after a filesystem identity change or a deliberate project restore when you want to retain the existing environment. Only run it after checking that the directory is the project you intend to use. The original matching regular \fB\&.oc\-box\-id\fR and host registry must still exist; repair cannot relocate a workspace or reconstruct missing registry/session data.
.PP
Exit the active launcher and stop its container first. Repair takes the workspace lock, checks container ownership, and refuses a running container. It updates only the registry's directory device/inode; project files, marker, container OS, private home, sessions, and saved settings are retained. It does not start or reset the container. An already matching identity needs no change.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box repair \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-resume
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Accepts only \fB\-\-project\fR or \fB\-\-workspace\fR selectors, besides help. With neither, use the current directory. With \fB\-\-workspace\fR, check the registered path and matching marker rather than the current directory.
.SS purge
.PP
Remove this workspace's labeled containers, owned volumes, owned networks, resume metadata, private recovery exports, and matching project marker. Exit its active launcher first. Shared base\-image caches and unrelated Podman resources are retained; cleanup does not use a global prune.
.PP
Default purge retains project files and host archive conversations. It attempts a final history sync while the project and container exist. If that sync fails, the private volume is retained for recovery. If the project was already deleted, there is no final sync; the last host archives remain.
.PP
\fB\-\-delete\-sessions\fR additionally deletes the recorded host archive copies, not an original host conversation imported into oc\-box. \fB\-\-all\fR additionally deletes the entire project directory. Project deletion requires the original directory identity and matching marker. Extra externally supplied mounts/volumes are not owned resources unless they bear this workspace's ownership label.
.PP
By default, enter the first eight characters of the workspace ID to confirm. \fB\-y\fR or \fB\-\-yes\fR skips this confirmation. These deletion flags apply only to purge.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box purge \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box purge \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-delete\-sessions
oc\-box purge \-\-project ~/devel/disposable \-\-all
oc\-box purge \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID \-\-all \-\-yes
.fam T
.fi
.SS list
.PP
List all registered workspaces with aligned headers: WORKSPACE ID, DISTRO, PROJECT, STATUS, and SESSION ID. No Podman invocation or container start is needed. Missing project directories remain listed so they can be purged by ID. STATUS means directory presence, not whether the container is running.
.PP
SESSION ID is the original container session used for resume, or \fBnone\fR\&. Local metadata is refreshed at startup/sync; a new conversation in a running client may not be reflected until the next sync or launch. With no workspaces, the headers are still printed. The list is global, not filtered by selectors; use \fB\-\-status\fR for a selected environment and machine\-readable output.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box list
.fam T
.fi
.SS distros
.PP
List supported distro names, image references, and the detected host default. No Podman invocation is required. This is a fixed supported image catalog; there is no custom\-image option. Selection is stored per workspace.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box distros
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-distro fedora
.fam T
.fi
.SS status
.PP
Alias of \fB\-\-status\fR\&. Report all registered environments unless \fB\-\-project\fR or \fB\-\-workspace\fR selects one. A selector must refer to an existing workspace. Status is read\-only and can run while OpenCode is active; it does not start, stop, or execute commands inside managed containers. Use \fB\-\-json\fR for numeric, machine\-readable output. See Resource reports for metric interpretation.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box status
oc\-box \-\-status \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box \-\-status \-\-json
.fam T
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.SS \-h, \-\-help
.PP
Print command choices, options, and short descriptions; exit without Podman. For example: \fBoc\-box \-\-help\fR\&.
.SS \-\-project PATH
.PP
Select an existing project directory; relative paths resolve from the host working directory. Quote paths containing spaces. For lifecycle commands the default is the current directory. For status, omission means all workspaces. The project must not be the host home, \fB/\:\fR, \fB/\:home\fR, the oc\-box registry, or a directory containing trusted launcher/state files. Colons, commas, and line breaks in the project path are unsupported because of Podman mount syntax.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-project "$HOME/devel/my project"
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-workspace ID
.PP
Select an existing workspace by its full 32\-character ID from \fBoc\-box list\fR\&. This works from another directory and allows cleanup after the project is deleted. Starting, syncing, or resetting still needs its original project directory. The identity marker and registry are not interchangeable with OpenCode session IDs. Example: \fBoc\-box \-\-status \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID\fR\&.
.SS \-\-status
.PP
Report CPU, RAM, storage, state, limits, and sessions without opening OpenCode. Equivalent to \fBstatus\fR\&. Only selectors and \fB\-\-json\fR apply. It cannot be combined with another command, session mode, resource change, or deletion action.
.SS \-\-json
.PP
Emit status as one JSON object with \fBsampled_at\fR and \fBworkspaces\fR\&. Sizes are numeric bytes and unavailable values are \fBnull\fR; notes explain partial results. Progress goes to stderr, not the JSON stream. Applies only to status.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-status \-\-json > status.json
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-about
.PP
Print oc\-box version, author Paulo Dias and email, OpenAI coding\-assistant contribution, GPL\-3.0\-or\-later licensing, and source commit. No Podman or workspace is required. Use alone, without lifecycle selectors/options.
.SS \-\-version
.PP
Print oc\-box's commit\-derived version and exit without Podman. The format is \fB0.1.N+gHASH\fR, with N the full\-history commit count. Each commit advances N; tracked uncommitted changes add \fB\&.dirty\fR\&. Untracked files do not. Installed copies retain their installation\-time identity in \fBbuild\-info.json\fR; reinstall to update it. This is separate from the host OpenCode version matched inside containers. Use alone, for example: \fBoc\-box \-\-version\fR\&.
.SS \-\-distro NAME
.PP
Choose a supported distro for a new environment. The default matches the host ID when supported; openSUSE Tumbleweed/Slowroll use Tumbleweed, other openSUSE variants use Leap, and unknown hosts fall back to Tumbleweed. The image is a distribution userland, not an exact clone of host packages or snapshot date. Changing an existing workspace requires \fBreset \-\-distro NAME\fR\&.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-distro tumbleweed
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-distro debian
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-new
.PP
Start a new root conversation while retaining the same container and files. Use with start (the default) or reset. Mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-resume\fR and \fB\-\-import\-session\fR\&. Example: \fBoc\-box \-\-project ~/\:devel/\:demo \-\-new\fR\&.
.SS \-\-resume
.PP
Explicitly request the normal resume behavior. Requires a registered workspace; if one exists but no root session has been recorded, OpenCode can start a new conversation. Use with start/reset. Mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-new\fR and \fB\-\-import\-session\fR\&. Example: \fBoc\-box \-\-project ~/\:devel/\:demo \-\-resume\fR\&.
.SS \-\-import\-session SESSION_ID
.PP
Copy a host conversation from the selected project into the private container database, verify the transcript, select the exact ID, and launch it. Start\-only; mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-new\fR and \fB\-\-resume\fR\&. Quit the source host client first to include its final saved turn. A subagent session cannot be imported as a root. The original host conversation is retained. See Sessions and recovery. An oc\-box host archive can also be imported into a fresh environment for that same project; the original registry and saved archive verify its project.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-import\-session ses_HOST_SESSION_ID
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-model PROVIDER/MODEL
.PP
Pass the provider/model choice to the OpenCode TUI on this launch. It is not a persistent oc\-box workspace setting. Use with start/reset; otherwise normal OpenCode provider/model preferences apply.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-model github\-copilot/gpt\-6.1\-sol
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-cpus NUMBER
.PP
Set the CPU quota in core units; fractions such as \fB2.5\fR are accepted by Podman. Default: \fB4\fR\&. Set at creation or reset. Existing containers, even stopped ones, require reset to change it; oc\-box does not call \fBpodman update\fR\&. Values are validated by Podman. \fB0\fR requests no CPU quota where supported.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-cpus 8
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-memory SIZE
.PP
Set the memory limit using Podman size syntax, such as \fB512m\fR, \fB8g\fR, or \fB16g\fR\&. Default: \fB8g\fR\&. Set at creation or reset; not a live update. The value is saved for future launches. Limits require delegated cgroup controllers. Podman validates the size and kernel support. \fB0\fR requests no memory limit where supported. There is no automatic project/volume disk quota.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-memory 16g
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-pids\-limit NUMBER
.PP
Set the container's process limit; default \fB2048\fR\&. oc\-box requires at least \fB32\fR\&. Set at creation or reset; changing an existing container requires reset. Example: \fBoc\-box reset \-\-project ~/\:devel/\:demo \-\-pids\-limit 4096\fR\&.
.SS \-\-device MAPPING
.PP
Add a persistent Podman mapping, repeatable. Supports \fB/\:host/\:device[:/\:container/\:device][:rwm]\fR and CDI \fBvendor/\:class=name\fR\&. New settings append to saved mappings; identical strings are deduplicated. Changing existing mappings requires reset. Use \fB\-\-clear\-devices\fR to replace or remove them. Host permissions, SELinux, and installed driver userland still apply; device mapping alone does not install an NPU/GPU SDK.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-device /dev/accel/accel0
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/gpu \-\-device /dev/dri/renderD128:rw
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-podman\-arg=\-\-OPTION[=VALUE]
.PP
Append one literal long\-option token to the Podman create command before the image. Repeat the flag for multiple options. Use \fB=\fR both after \fB\-\-podman\-arg\fR and within valued Podman options; this is not a shell command string or a space\-split list. Values containing spaces remain one argument when quoted. Saved options persist across launches/resets; identical strings are deduplicated. Remove old/conflicting options with \fB\-\-clear\-podman\-args\fR when replacing them.
.PP
The following are reserved by oc\-box: \fB\-\-name\fR, \fB\-\-label\fR, \fB\-\-label\-file\fR, \fB\-\-cidfile\fR, \fB\-\-replace\fR, \fB\-\-rm\fR, \fB\-\-entrypoint\fR, \fB\-\-workdir\fR, \fB\-\-user\fR, \fB\-\-rootfs\fR, \fB\-\-pod\fR, and \fB\-\-pod\-id\-file\fR\&. Use oc\-box's dedicated CPU, memory, and process flags instead of forwarding their Podman equivalents. Options are for \fBpodman create\fR, not global Podman flags or arguments after the image.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \e
  \-\-podman\-arg=\-\-shm\-size=1g \e
  \-\-podman\-arg=\-\-ulimit=memlock=\-1:\-1
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Extra devices, mounts, capabilities, host networking, and security options extend the default boundary as requested by the host operator. Podman and the host's rootless permissions determine which settings are available.
.SS \-\-clear\-devices
.PP
Clear all saved \fB\-\-device\fR mappings before adding any new ones on this command. Use during reset for an existing container. It does not remove a device passed as a raw Podman option; use \fB\-\-clear\-podman\-args\fR for that list.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-clear\-devices
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-clear\-devices \-\-device /dev/accel/accel1
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-clear\-podman\-args
.PP
Clear saved additional Podman options before appending replacements. Use during reset for an existing container. It leaves the dedicated device list unchanged.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-clear\-podman\-args \e
  \-\-podman\-arg=\-\-shm\-size=2g
.fam T
.fi
.SS \-\-delete\-sessions
.PP
Purge\-only. Delete recorded host archive session copies as well as environment resources. Project files remain unless \fB\-\-all\fR is used. It never deletes an original host source conversation merely because that conversation was imported into this workspace. Example: \fBoc\-box purge \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID \-\-delete\-sessions\fR\&.
.SS \-\-all
.PP
Purge\-only. Remove environment resources, recorded host session archives, metadata, and the entire original project directory, including untracked files. The directory identity and marker must still match. Example: \fBoc\-box purge \-\-project ~/\:devel/\:disposable \-\-all\fR\&.
.SS \-y, \-\-yes
.PP
Skip purge's ID\-prefix confirmation. Purge\-only; it does not answer OpenCode questions or bypass permissions on other commands. Example: \fBoc\-box purge \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID \-\-all \-\-yes\fR\&.
.SH LIFECYCLE AND PERSISTENCE
.PP
.nf
.fam C
Operation              Project   Private home/sessions   Container OS
Exit OpenCode          kept      kept; archive sync     kept; stopped
stop                   kept      kept                   kept; stopped
sync                   kept      kept; archive sync     kept; stopped
reset                  kept      kept, same session ID  rebuilt
purge                  kept      deleted                deleted
purge \-\-delete\-sessions kept      deleted                deleted
purge \-\-all            deleted   deleted                deleted
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Default purge retains host archive copies; \fB\-\-delete\-sessions\fR and \fB\-\-all\fR remove recorded host archive copies. Imported source host conversations remain. Shared base images are retained by all operations. Stop/reset do not checkpoint running processes. Packages installed in the private home survive reset; system\-wide packages do not. No command currently manages Btrfs snapshots.
.PP
CPU, RAM, process, distro, and mapping changes require reset for existing containers. A stop/start alone reuses the old create\-time settings. Avoid changing resources directly through Podman if metadata consistency is required. An active launcher holds a nonblocking workspace lock; start, sync, reset, repair, and purge require that launcher to exit. Read\-only list/status and explicit stop can run from a second terminal. A crash releases the lock when its process exits; an orphan still running can retain it.
.SH DISTRIBUTION IMAGES
.PP
.nf
.fam C
Name         Image
tumbleweed   registry.opensuse.org/opensuse/tumbleweed:latest
leap         registry.opensuse.org/opensuse/leap:latest
ubuntu       docker.io/library/ubuntu:24.04
debian       docker.io/library/debian:stable
fedora       registry.fedoraproject.org/fedora:latest
arch         docker.io/library/archlinux:base
alpine       docker.io/library/alpine:latest
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Aliases: \fBopensuse\fR and \fBopensuse\-tumbleweed\fR select \fBtumbleweed\fR; \fBopensuse\-leap\fR selects \fBleap\fR; \fBarchlinux\fR selects \fBarch\fR\&. \fBoc\-box distros\fR reports the actual compiled catalog. Images are pulled when missing, not automatically refreshed to a newer remote tag on every launch.
.SH SESSIONS AND RECOVERY
.PP
The live database is private container storage. The normal host database is not mounted. Normal exit and \fBsync\fR export root conversations using supported mainline commands, then import namespaced host archive IDs with titles prefixed \fB[oc\-box project\-name]\fR\&. Repeated sync refreshes the archive copy. Resume uses the original container session, not that archive ID.
.PP
This is conversation\-history sync, not live replication or a backup against disk loss. Host archives omit snapshot stores, separate subagent transcripts, running processes, installed packages, and files outside the exported transcript. New work in a host archive is not merged back automatically. Container/home storage normally survives a client crash or reboot; the last uncommitted turn can still be lost. Retry missed archival with \fBoc\-box sync\fR\&.
.PP
For \fB\-\-import\-session\fR, quit the source client first. Its project must match the selected directory. A private source export is saved in \fBimports/\:SESSION_ID.json\fR under the workspace registry. oc\-box imports into the container, verifies every message/part, then launches that exact ID. Mainline's removal of obsolete nonpending tool \fBstate.raw\fR buffers is accepted, but tool inputs/outputs, pending raw buffers, text, IDs, and metadata remain checked.
.PP
Do not routinely repeat the import flag. An already verified ID is not merged or overwritten. If verification was interrupted/rejected, retry the same import after updating the launcher: an existing session may be recovered by comparison with the original saved export, without reimporting over container work. Differences outside the known normalization stop launch and retain both copies.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-import\-session ses_HOST_SESSION_ID
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box sync \-\-project ~/devel/demo
.fam T
.fi
.PP
The source\-tree diagnostic prints counts and differing field names, not text:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
python3 tools/diagnose_import.py \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID \-\-session SESSION_ID
.fam T
.fi
.SS Recover history after replacing a workspace marker
.PP
Removing \fB\&.oc\-box\-id\fR and starting again creates a fresh environment; it does not delete the old private volume, registry, or host archives. To continue a previously synced conversation in the new environment, exit its active client, find the host archive title prefixed \fB[oc\-box project\-name]\fR, and import its ID:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
opencode \-\-pure session list
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-import\-session ses_ARCHIVE_ID
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Mainline records host archive imports against oc\-box's private \fBcontroller\fR directory. oc\-box verifies the archive's namespaced ID against that workspace, its original host registry, and its saved \fBhistory/\:SESSION_ID.json\fR before restoring the project directory for transfer. Both saved records must identify the selected project. The current host transcript is copied, retaining any later host archive work; the host session and saved archive are not changed. The archive title alone cannot authorize a different project.
.PP
Use the import flag once, then resume normally. If the original registry or saved export is missing, restore it before retrying. Host history contains only successfully synced turns; the old private volume may contain newer work. Keep the original workspace resources until recovery is complete. Importing history does not copy installed packages, snapshots, or other private\-home files.
.SH DEVICES AND HOST PERMISSIONS
.PP
The host user must be able to access the device. Host supplementary groups are not automatically preserved; where group access is required, use installed crun and \fB\-\-group\-add=keep\-groups\fR\&. A same\-named group inside the container is not the host group. Newly acquired groups require a fresh host login/process with those groups. Driver\-specific SDKs must be installed inside the container.
.PP
Before creating/recreating device\-enabled containers, oc\-box checks requested host read/write access and SELinux's device boolean, including \fB/\:usr/\:sbin\fR utilities that may be absent from PATH. Warnings are advisory; oc\-box does not run sudo or change host policy. The policy warning is skipped when device access is already allowed, SELinux is not enforcing, or the container explicitly bypasses labeling. CDI devices receive the SELinux check, not a host\-path check.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/npu \e
  \-\-device /dev/accel/accel0 \e
  \-\-podman\-arg=\-\-runtime=crun \e
  \-\-podman\-arg=\-\-group\-add=keep\-groups
.fam T
.fi
.PP
SELinux can deny access even when a mapped device name is visible with \fB\-?????????\fR attributes. From the host, inspect policy and permissions:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
ls \-ldZ /dev/accel
ls \-lZ /dev/accel/accel0
id
/usr/sbin/getenforce
/usr/sbin/getsebool container_use_devices
test \-r /dev/accel/accel0 && test \-w /dev/accel/accel0
.fam T
.fi
.PP
If the user has access and enforcing SELinux has the device boolean off, Podman's documented host\-wide policy setting is:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
sudo /usr/sbin/setsebool \-P container_use_devices on
.fam T
.fi
.PP
It permits device labels subject to mappings and Unix permissions; it does not map extra devices or disable SELinux. It persists and applies immediately, including to running containers, without reset/reboot. A memlock problem does not explain failure to read device attributes. Once access works, inspect the driver and limits independently inside the container:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
ls \-l /dev/accel/accel0
prlimit \-\-pid $$ \-\-memlock
xrt\-smi examine
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Requested memlock limits are still subject to the host user's hard limit and kernel/runtime rules; passing a ulimit does not grant host privileges.
.SH RESOURCE REPORTS
.PP
Status collects running state, CPU, RAM/limit, PIDs, uptime, configured limits, image, storage driver, restart/exit/OOM/health information when available, and the latest saved session and archive IDs. Device mappings and saved raw Podman options are included. No container commands run during collection.
.PP
Storage is split into writable OS layer, private home volume, and project. The rootfs size including shared image layers is separate. Filesystem used/free space and block\-I/O counters are shown. CPU is a short sample where 100% is one core; stopped containers have no live sample. Missing/unavailable values are not reported as zero. Rootless pasta network counters are omitted as unreliable.
.PP
Directory scans count allocated blocks without following symlinks or crossing filesystems, with a 15\-second timeout per directory. Private\-volume scans use \fBpodman unshare\fR on the host, not container exec. Very large caches may take time or report unavailable size. Shared image layers must not be summed as unique storage. Btrfs compression, reflinks, snapshots, and shared extents make these estimates different from exclusive physical usage. There is no disk quota.
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-status
oc\-box \-\-status \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID \-\-json > workspace\-status.json
.fam T
.fi
.SH CONFIGURATION AND ENVIRONMENT
.PP
On launch, global OpenCode configuration, agents, skills, local plugins, and MCP settings are copied into private storage; they are not host mounts. Project configuration runs natively inside the container. Removed host plugins are removed from the copy. Container dependency caches and generated SDK locks are reused when host dependency inputs and runtime version are unchanged. Cold plugin resolution has its own timed setup stage before the TUI opens.
.PP
The generated profile selects Bash and enables native formatters/LSP when not explicitly configured. A container\-only plugin sets Build permissions to allow all. Plan/subagent and plain host permissions are unchanged. Other copied plugins may have independent checks. Changes require exiting and restarting OpenCode; config is not hot\-reloaded. Host\-only executables must be installed inside the container. Host provider/MCP auth is copied when available; refreshed container auth is not written back to host auth.
.PP
HTTP endpoint keys in global config using localhost, 127.0.0.1, or ::1 are rewritten to \fBhost.containers.internal\fR\&. OAuth redirect URIs and shell commands are not rewritten. Container localhost remains local to the container.
.SS Environment variables
.PP
\fBOC_BOX_STATE_HOME\fR overrides the host workspace registry root. Otherwise it is \fB$XDG_STATE_HOME/\:oc\-box\fR, defaulting to \fB~/\:.local/\:state/\:oc\-box\fR\&. Use the same registry when looking up existing workspace IDs; changing it does not migrate old resources automatically.
.PP
\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR selects the source global OpenCode/oc\-box configuration; default \fB~/\:.config\fR\&. \fBXDG_DATA_HOME\fR selects the normal host OpenCode data root; default \fB~/\:.local/\:share\fR\&. The installer itself uses fixed \fB~/\:.local\fR paths. Container XDG paths use the private home, not host XDG directories.
.PP
Variables ending \fB_API_KEY\fR or \fB_TOKEN\fR, plus \fBGH_TOKEN\fR, \fBGITHUB_TOKEN\fR, terminal color/language settings, and \fBLEMONADE_BASE_URL\fR are passed when set. Add other uppercase environment names in host configuration:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
{
  "pass_env": ["MY_MCP_SETTING", "MY_PROVIDER_SECRET"]
}
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Save that as \fB~/\:.config/\:oc\-box/\:config.json\fR (or its XDG equivalent). Only values present in the host environment are copied. Forwarded HTTP localhost URLs are mapped to the host\-service name. The default \fBLEMONADE_BASE_URL\fR is \fBhttp:/\:/\:host.containers.internal:13305/\:api/\:v1\fR\&. \fBTERM\fR defaults to \fBxterm\-256color\fR\&. Host PATH, shell startup scripts, proxy variables, engine sockets, and SSH agents are not forwarded by default.
.PP
Forwarded credential/custom environment values are passed through the Podman client's environment, not embedded in its process arguments. They are still accessible to container processes and same\-user environment inspection. Copied configuration and auth can also contain secrets; only supply credentials the agent should use. See SECURITY.md for the documented trust boundary.
.PP
\fBOPENCODE_AUTH_CONTENT\fR can supply provider auth to copy into the container. The launcher sets \fBOC_BOX_CONTAINER=1\fR, \fBOPENCODE_DISABLE_SANDBOX=1\fR, and \fBOPENCODE_DISABLE_AUTOUPDATE=1\fR inside it, plus its private profile/config. OpenCode's existing Bubblewrap plugin is disabled only for this launch; Podman is the environment boundary. No OpenCode fork is installed.
.SH FILES
.SS Host installation
.PP
\fB~/\:.local/\:bin/\:oc\-box\fR is the launcher. \fB~/\:.local/\:share/\:oc\-box/\:\fR contains installed Python sources, documentation, LICENSE, NOTICE, and embedded \fBbuild\-info.json\fR\&. The source checkout and installation are independent; reinstall after updates. \fB~/\:.local/\:share/\:man/\:man1/\:oc\-box.1\fR and \fB~/\:.local/\:share/\:info/\:oc\-box.info\fR are the installed manuals. Installation requires no man/Info build tools.
.SS Host registry and archives
.PP
\fB~/\:.local/\:state/\:oc\-box/\:workspaces/\:ID/\:workspace.json\fR holds directory identity, container/volume names, distro, limits, and saved Podman/device settings. \fBsessions.json\fR holds session IDs and archive associations. \fBimports/\:\fR holds private source exports; \fBhistory/\:\fR holds private host archive JSON files. \fBlock\fR serializes mutating commands. These files are outside the project and not mounted into the container. Transcript files may contain private content.
.PP
\fB\&.oc\-box\-id\fR in the project associates the directory with its registry. Do not edit or copy it to authorize a different mount. Identity checks include the canonical path and directory device/inode. Deleting and recreating the same path produces a new workspace rather than silently reusing the old environment. An explicitly requested \fBrepair\fR can accept the current identity at the same path with the original matching marker, retaining the existing environment.
.PP
The normal host OpenCode database, usually under \fB~/\:.local/\:share/\:opencode/\:\fR, contains archive copies. Old installations may retain one\-time relocation backups under \fB~/\:.local/\:state/\:oc\-box/\:migrations/\:\fR; these are private data, not workspace resources or release artifacts, and later workspace purge leaves them intact. The original development\-only relocation helper is retired.
.SS Container data
.PP
The home volume is \fBoc\-box\-home\-ID\fR, mounted at the host user's home pathname. OpenCode data lives under its \fB\&.local/\:share/\:opencode/\:\fR\&. Copied configuration and generated \fBoc\-box\-profile.json\fR live under \fB\&.config/\:opencode/\:\fR\&. The matching OpenCode executable is \fB/\:opt/\:oc\-box/\:runtime/\:bin/\:opencode\fR; Node/npm, Python/uv, Bash, Git, ripgrep, curl, archive tools, and PDF tools are provided by baseline setup. Additional system tools belong to the container OS layer.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
\fB0\fR means successful informational/lifecycle work or normal TUI exit. A TUI launch normally returns OpenCode/Podman's exit code. oc\-box validation, backend, setup, and sync failures return \fB1\fR; a successful TUI followed by failed history sync also returns \fB1\fR and retains container data. Argument syntax errors return \fB2\fR\&. A Python KeyboardInterrupt handled by the launcher returns \fB130\fR\&. An exception during final cleanup can also cause a nonzero result.
.PP
Warnings about host devices/SELinux do not themselves make creation fail. Podman/driver errors still propagate. A nonzero status is not permission to purge data; recover or inspect the existing environment first.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Everyday start and resume
.PP
.nf
.fam C
mkdir \-p ~/devel/demo
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-new
.fam T
.fi
.PP
The second launch resumes work. A new conversation does not reset files or installed tools. Exit OpenCode normally to synchronize history and stop it.
.SS Inspect from a second terminal
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box list
oc\-box \-\-status
oc\-box \-\-status \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-json > status.json
podman ps \-a \-\-filter label=io.oc\-box.managed=true
.fam T
.fi
.SS Grow an existing environment without losing progress
.PP
Exit its client before reset. Preserve source, private home, and session IDs, but reinstall project\-specific system packages afterward:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-cpus 8 \-\-memory 16g \-\-pids\-limit 4096
.fam T
.fi
.SS Continue a host session and recover after a crash
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-import\-session ses_HOST_SESSION_ID
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box sync \-\-project ~/devel/demo
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Import once; normal launches use the saved container session afterward.
.SS Replace persistent hardware settings
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box reset \-\-project ~/devel/npu \-\-clear\-devices \-\-device /dev/accel/accel0 \e
  \-\-clear\-podman\-args \-\-podman\-arg=\-\-runtime=crun \e
  \-\-podman\-arg=\-\-group\-add=keep\-groups
.fam T
.fi
.SS Remove a disposable project completely
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box purge \-\-project ~/devel/disposable \-\-all
oc\-box list
.fam T
.fi
.PP
If the directory was deleted manually, use its workspace ID instead:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box list
oc\-box purge \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID \-\-all
.fam T
.fi
.PP
The last command deletes its remaining managed resources and recorded host archives; an unrelated replacement directory will not be deleted.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.SS Directory identity changed after cleanup or a filesystem change
.PP
Deleting host \fB~/\:.cache\fR alone does not remove oc\-box's registry or private session volume and should not change the project directory identity. The "Project directory identity changed" error means its recorded device/inode differs from the current directory. Older launchers describe this as "This directory replaced the original workspace" and recommend removing the marker. A device number can change after a filesystem remount; a restored or recreated directory can have a different inode.
.PP
Keep the marker to retain the association with the existing environment. Update/reinstall the launcher, check that the project at the registered path is correct, and exit its active client. Then run from a host terminal:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
oc\-box repair \-\-project ~/devel/demo
oc\-box \-\-project ~/devel/demo \-\-resume
.fam T
.fi
.PP
If the container is still running, use \fBoc\-box stop \-\-workspace WORKSPACE_ID\fR and retry repair. Repair preserves the container, private volume, and session IDs. It does not recover storage that was deleted separately.
.PP
If the marker has no matching host registry, check \fBOC_BOX_STATE_HOME\fR and \fBXDG_STATE_HOME\fR and restore the original registry before resuming. The default registry is \fB~/\:.local/\:state/\:oc\-box\fR, separate from \fB~/\:.cache\fR\&.
.SS Missing delegated CPU or memory controllers
.PP
On some openSUSE hosts a vendor user\-manager drop\-in clears delegation. Startup then fails on \fBmemory.swap.max\fR or missing cgroup controllers despite kernel support. The launcher reports this before starting the container. From a host terminal, substitute your numeric UID for 1000:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
sudo systemctl edit user@1000.service \-\-drop\-in=90\-oc\-box\-delegate.conf
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Save the override:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
[Service]
Delegate=cpu cpuset io memory pids
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Run \fBsudo systemctl daemon\-reload\fR\&. If the current user manager still lacks the controllers, fully end/restart it through a fresh login or reboot. A reboot is not a blanket Podman installation requirement. Check effective delegation in \fB/\:sys/\:fs/\:cgroup/\:user.slice/\:user\-1000.slice/\:user@1000.service/\:cgroup.controllers\fR\&. Retry the existing workspace without reset or purge. Do not edit vendor files.
.SS Project mount denied by SELinux
.PP
New mounts use Podman's shared \fB:z\fR project relabeling. Older mounts can exist but deny file access. Relabel only the project from the host:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
chcon \-R \-t container_file_t /path/to/project
.fam T
.fi
.PP
Resume normally; no session reimport/reset is needed. Device\-node labeling is separate: use the device\-policy diagnostics in Devices and host permissions.
.SS Slow first launch or blank terminal
.PP
Cold creation performs package and plugin downloads. Timed stage output and the temporary TUI loading line indicate progress. Warm starts retain compatible dependency state. Host SDK manifests older than runtime are normalized inside the copied config, not by modifying host configuration. Preserve the environment when diagnosing a wait; reset discards the OS cache and can repeat cold setup.
.SS Active workspace lock or failed import
.PP
Exit the active launcher before start/sync/reset/purge. Status and stop remain available in another terminal. Do not delete the lock file to bypass a live process. For a failed import, retry the same flag with an updated launcher; recovery uses the saved export without overwriting existing container work. For a failed archive sync, use \fBsync\fR; the original live DB remains in its volume. A deleted private volume cannot be recovered from session metadata alone.
.SH INSTALLATION AND MANUALS
.PP
Requires Linux, Python 3.10 or later, rootless Podman with pasta networking, delegated cgroup CPU/memory/PIDs controllers, and host OpenCode 1.x. Session import/export compatibility was checked with OpenCode 1.19.5. Other major OpenCode versions are rejected rather than assuming database/CLI compatibility. Podman must work from the regular host user, not through sudo. Network access is needed for initial distribution, runtime, and dependency downloads.
.PP
Install from the source checkout as your host user:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
python3 install.py
oc\-box \-\-version
man oc\-box
info oc\-box
.fam T
.fi
.PP
The installer copies prebuilt manual files and does not require makeinfo, groff, man, or info. Reading them needs the host reader; on openSUSE those are the \fBman\fR and \fBinfo\fR packages. It updates only the user's Info index when an \fBinstall\-info\fR utility is available, not any system index. Ensure \fB~/\:.local/\:bin\fR is on PATH. If local documentation directories are not searched:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
MANPATH="$HOME/.local/share/man:" man oc\-box
INFOPATH="$HOME/.local/share/info:" info oc\-box
man "$HOME/.local/share/man/man1/oc\-box.1"
info \-\-file "$HOME/.local/share/info/oc\-box.info"
.fam T
.fi
.PP
The full CLI reference is \fBdocs/\:cli.md\fR; the source tree includes generated \fBdocs/\:oc\-box.1\fR, \fBdocs/\:oc\-box.texi\fR, and \fBdocs/\:oc\-box.info\fR\&. Maintainers regenerate them with \fBpython3 tools/\:build_docs.py\fR; that build requires GNU makeinfo. \fBpython3 tools/\:build_docs.py \-\-check\fR checks the generated man/Texinfo source. End users do not need to rebuild documentation to install it.
